> You step onto the landing. The corridor opens onto a broad landing where a wide staircase spills down into darkness, its banister worn smooth by centuries of hands. This is the stair you heard creaking; it settles now, still and watchful, as if it had been going somewhere and thought better of it. A tall window frames the mere below, black water pricked with moonlight, and a Vigil Lamp wakes on the wall to lay a warm coin of light at your feet. The stair descends steeply down, an archway leads east into shadow, and a smaller door stands west.
Exits: down, east, south, west.
> You step through into the portrait gallery. The archway gives onto a long gallery where dead headmasters hang in gilt frames, their painted faces gone to shadow save where a waking Vigil Lamp catches an eye or a folded hand. The air is close with old varnish and dust, and somewhere down the row a canvas creaks in its frame as a painted figure shifts to watch you pass. A run of tall windows on the south wall lets the mere-light lie in cold bars across the floorboards; the gallery runs on east into deeper dark, and a low door opens north.
Exits: east, north, west.
